    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The $\\chi^{2}$ for goodness of fit to Hardy-Weinberg proportions is 1.11. There is one degree of freedom for this test: 4 (to start with) - 1 (for fixing the total at 6313) - 1 (for estimating $\\hat{p}_{1}$ from the data) - 1 (for estimating $\\hat{p}_{2}$ from the data); note that a degree of freedom is not deducted for estimating $\\hat{p}_{3}$ because $\\hat{p}_{3} = 1 - \\hat{p}_{1} - \\hat{p}_{2}$. (More generally, when there are $n$ alleles and $m$ possible phenotypes ($m > n$), then the number of degrees of freedom in a chi-square test for HWE is $m - 1 - (n - 1) = m - n$.) For a $\\chi^{2}$ of 1.11 with one degree of freedom, the associated probability from Figure 2.3 is about 0.29, and so the Iowa City population gives no evidence against Hardy-Weinberg proportions for this gene.",
      "source_page": 83,
      "source_pages": [
        83
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "PopGen_chapter2:p025:b3"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
