      "content_sha256": "f453e46740b0d4beb1f832eeda3c5d8bae27c0d11918ae0ef83eccccfb4d817f",
      "source_locator_mode": "exact",
      "inline_styles": []
    },
    {
      "type": "problem",
      "content": "PROBLEM 2.7 In a sample of 1617 Spanish Basques, the numbers of A, B, O, and AB blood types observed were 724, 110, 763, and 20, respectively. The best estimates of allele frequency are $ \\hat{p}_1 = 0.2661 $ (for $ I^A $), $ \\hat{p}_2 = 0.0411 $ (for $ I^B $), and $ \\hat{p}_3 = 0.6928 $ (for $ I^O $). Calculate the expected numbers of the four phenotypes and carry out a $ \\chi^2 $ test for goodness of fit to the Hardy-Weinberg expectations.",
      "source_page": 83,
      "source_pages": [
        83
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "PopGen_chapter2:p025:b4",
        "PopGen_chapter2:p025:b6"
      ],
