    {
      "type": "answer",
      "content": "ANSWER The expected numbers of A, B, O, and AB are 710.7, 94.8, 776.1, and 35.4, respectively. The $ \\chi^{2} $ equals 9.61 with one degree of freedom, for which the corresponding probability is 0.002. Because a deviation as large or larger than that observed would be expected by chance in only 0.002 samples (that is, about 1 in 500), there is very good reason to reject the hypothesis that the genotypes are in Hardy-Weinberg proportions in this population. The reason for the discrepancy is not known. One likely possibility is migration into the population by people with allele frequencies that are significantly different from those among the Basques themselves.",
